The provinces of the former Hachinawa are large because much of their territory was not under the republic's administration when the boundaries were drawn. The largest, Hashawa, contains chiefdoms that had never been subject to the colony, and it was constituted as a single province rather than divided because its settled districts were few.

The provinces of the west and centre are small for the opposite reason. Their boundaries were drawn where the administration's authority was complete and where the Tianist population was concentrated, and the equal representation of provinces in the Senate converts that concentration into a majority.

== General Assembly ==

{{Main|General Assembly of Sinchew}}

The lower house was the General Assembly, elected by universal male suffrage with members holding two-year terms and no property qualification for the vote. Seats were allocated at large from the provinces.

The two-year term made the Assembly the most frequently renewed body in the government. The constitution assigned it legislation, taxation, the appointment of the president, the appointment of the judges of the High Court, and the creation of agencies charged with executive tasks. No legislation was enacted by the Assembly alone: ordinary laws required the agreement of both chambers, and constitutional amendments required a majority of the Assembly and two-thirds of the Senate.

The franchise was male. Women were excluded, and the exclusion was maintained for longer than any other provision was left unamended.

== Senate ==

The upper house was the Senate, composed of two members from each province, elected by the provincial assemblies.

The Senate could refuse legislation, and its refusal was final. An ordinary law required the agreement of both chambers, and the Assembly could not enact one over the Senate's objection. The chamber therefore gated all legislation rather than only constitutional change, and every enactment of the state passed the provinces' representatives.

The Senate was therefore elected at one remove from the population, and the provincial assemblies that chose its members were themselves elected within boundaries drawn by the Tianist minority. The arrangement gave that minority control of the Senate through two mechanisms rather than one: the equal representation of provinces regardless of size, and the smaller electorates of the small provinces, whose assemblies are easier for a concentrated class to carry.

== President ==

The head of state was a President elected by the General Assembly. The president was not elected by the population and held no mandate independent of the Assembly that appointed him.

The presidency was not a chief executive. The constitution established no cabinet and vested no general executive power in the office. The president was the head of state, conducted foreign affairs, and administered the affairs of the agencies the Assembly created. Domestic administration was carried on through those agencies rather than through a government answerable to the president.

The office was therefore the Assembly's instrument in two fields - relations with other states, and the coordination of the bodies the Assembly had itself established - and had no independent authority in the third, which was the government of the country.

== Executive agencies ==

The General Assembly held the power to create agencies charged with executive tasks. The departments of the state were established by the Assembly in this way, and the constitution places no limit on the number or kind of agency the Assembly may create.

The arrangement places the executive inside the legislature rather than beside it. There is no cabinet, no prime minister and no ministry deriving its authority from the president; the bodies that administer the country derive their authority from the Assembly that made them, and the president coordinates them without commanding them.

== Defence ==

The constitution established a standing professional military comprising an army, a navy and a ranger corps.

{{Main|Armed Forces of Sinchew}}

The rangers were constituted as the force responsible for the interior. Their task was the maintenance of order in the hinterlands and the forests, and they were trained to traverse mountain and jungle and to operate in small units in difficult country. The corps was formed for the territory the republic did not govern: the chiefdoms of the largest provinces, the forested interior, and the frontiers where no civil administration reached.

The military is subordinate to the [[General Assembly of Sinchew|General Assembly]], which raises it, funds it and may direct it. The president is its civilian commander, and holds the command alongside the conduct of foreign affairs and the administration of the Assembly's agencies. The subordination was written with the conduct of the colonial navy in mind: in 1711 an admiral of the [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] fleet stationed at [[Tamsui]] had despatched a squadron against [[Sant Cristòfor]] without authorisation and begun the [[First War of the Isthmus]], and imperial approval was granted after the fact. The constitution gives the president the conduct of foreign affairs and gives the Assembly the power to raise and direct the forces, so that a commander cannot commit the state to war.

== High Court ==

The constitution established a High Court with inferior courts beneath it. The judges of the High Court were appointed by the General Assembly and held lifetime positions.

The lifetime tenure made the judiciary the only branch not subject to the Assembly's renewal, and it placed the court outside the two-year cycle at the same time as it placed its members there by election of the Assembly.

The High Court determined the constitutionality of laws and of the Assembly's procedures, and acted as a check on the Assembly. Its jurisdiction extended to the question of whether an enactment or a proceeding was consistent with the constitution, and the court's determination settled the matter.

The function is the more consequential in this constitution because the state has no executive independent of the legislature. There is no president with a veto, no cabinet whose confidence the Assembly must retain, and no second chamber that can refuse legislation it dislikes; the [[Senate of Sinchew|Senate]] can reject a bill, but the Assembly may reintroduce it. The High Court is the only body able to hold an enactment void, and it is therefore the principal check on the Assembly's power.

=== Challenges ===

The constitution distinguishes two kinds of bill. A bill may be an amendment to the constitution, in which case it requires a majority of the Assembly and two-thirds of the Senate. A bill may also be an ordinary law, in which case it is subject to the constitution and requires the agreement of both chambers, as all legislation does.

An ordinary law is presumed to be constitutional. A person may challenge a law before the High Court and ask the court to determine its constitutionality, and the court decides the question; but a law that is not challenged stands, and a challenge must establish that the enactment is inconsistent with the constitution. The presumption places the burden on the person challenging the law rather than on the Assembly that passed it.

The court's power is therefore exercised only when it is invoked. The High Court cannot review an enactment of its own motion, and no officer of the state is required to bring a law before it. A law that is inconsistent with the constitution remains in force until someone with reason to object asks the court to say so.

The court's composition places the appointment of the Assembly's own check in the Assembly's hands: the judges are chosen by the body whose acts they review, and they hold office for life once chosen.

=== Removal of judges ===

A judge of the High Court may be tried by the General Assembly and removed. Removal requires a two-thirds vote of both chambers - two-thirds of the General Assembly and two-thirds of the Senate.

The threshold is higher than the one the constitution sets for its own amendment. An amendment requires a majority of the Assembly and two-thirds of the Senate; the removal of a judge requires two-thirds of each. The instrument is harder to alter in its judicial clause than in any other part, and the judges are therefore more firmly entrenched than the constitutional text that creates them.

The Senate's share of both thresholds is the same twenty-two votes in a chamber of thirty-two, and the body that must consent to a judge's removal is the same body whose composition an amendment could alter.

== Religion ==

The constitution provided for religious freedom and established no state religion.

The provision ended the position the [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] population had held under the colonial administration, which had been Tianist and had expelled the Baiduists of mainland Zong on religious grounds in 1606. It also ended the establishment of Tianism, which had been the religion of the colony's administration and of the Zong crown.

The provision was the point on which the two bodies that wrote the constitution agreed most closely. The [[Union of Free Peoples]] was Baiduist and Hahkina and had been subject to a religious establishment; the [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly|Liberal Assembly]] was Tianist and had lost the establishment that had favoured it. Neither had reason to maintain one.

== Language ==

The constitution named no official language. It recognised a set of languages for common use and provided that proceedings at the national level would be conducted in Zong, and that each province may designate its own secondary languages for provincial government.

The provision left the Union's three constituent peoples governed in the language of one of them at the national level and permitted the others to govern themselves in their own. Zong is the language of the former colony and of the Baiduists and Sitonglan; Hachina is the language of the former Hachinawa; and the Mahik and Apyãwa peoples retain their own.

== Rights ==

The constitution enumerated protections against the government: freedom of the press, freedom of assembly, {{w|habeas corpus}}, and freedom of religion.

The provisions were sought by both founding constituencies. The [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly|Liberal Assembly]] was a body of landowners who had been suppressed after the rebellion of 1717, and the Baiduist population had been expelled from Zong on religious grounds in 1606 and governed by an administration of another faith for a century.

== Land ==

The constitution did not redistribute land. It guaranteed the protection of land held by its owners against interference by the government, and provided that the government may purchase land at fair rates.

The provision settled the question left open by the revolution. The land settlement of the former Hachinawa had transferred the province's cultivable ground to Zong settlers through legal adjudication between 1662 and 1680, and the constitution confirmed the titles that transfer had created. The Hahkina and Baiduist constituencies of the [[Union of Free Peoples]], which had lost that ground, obtained the guarantee against further dispossession rather than the return of what had been taken.

== Debt ==

The constitution repudiated the colonial debts. The slave-backed loans issued by the colonial government in 1711 were voided, and the state assumed no obligation to compensate the landowners who had contributed enslaved labour to the war.

The landowning class accepted the repudiation as part of the settlement. It had been the immediate cause of the revolution: the Zong court had refused to honour the loans in 1717 and had required the colony to emancipate the remainder at its own cost, and the colony's leading class had joined the revolution rather than the crown.

== National and provincial powers ==

The constitution separated the powers of the national and provincial governments. The national government is bound by the constitution and holds the powers the document enumerates. The provinces hold the powers the constitution does not mention, and each has an assembly of its own to exercise them.

The arrangement is a residual-powers settlement: the national government is a government of enumerated authority and the provinces are governments of general authority. It was the provision the former imperial province sought, and it is the reason the abolition of the province's autonomy was accepted — the assembly of the former Hachinawa was dissolved into the General Assembly, and every province received an assembly of the same kind.

== Royal house ==

The royal house of the former Hachinawa was abolished. The dynasty that had reigned since before the first Zong sighting of the coast in 1458, and which had administered the annexed province until the death of [[Kayatsi]] in 1678, ceased to be a house of any constitutional status.

The state is named for the Hahkina among its constituent peoples, and it contains no office for the house that had ruled them.

== Amendment ==

The constitution was drafted over eight months and written to be amended. An amendment requires a majority of the [[General Assembly of Sinchew|General Assembly]] and two-thirds of the [[Senate of Sinchew|Senate]].

The threshold exceeds the strength of any single grouping in the Senate. Two-thirds of the thirty-two seats is twenty-one and one third, and therefore twenty-two votes. The ten Tianist-aligned provinces of the west and centre hold twenty seats; [[Tiengo]] and the five provinces of the former [[Hachinawa]] hold twelve.

No grouping can therefore amend the constitution alone or block an amendment alone. The Tianist-aligned provinces are two votes short of the threshold and the Baiduist and native provinces are ten short of it. An amendment requires the agreement of the Tianist grouping and at least one further province, or of the Baiduist and native grouping and five of the ten western provinces.

The two-vote margin has made the smaller western provinces the deciding votes in every amendment attempted since 1736. The removal of a judge of the High Court requires more than an amendment does: two-thirds of both chambers,, so that the judicial clause is the most entrenched provision of the instrument. Each holds two senators, so an amendment turns on the inclinations of one or two provinces rather than on either bloc's programme.

The requirement is the counterpart of the [[Union of Free Peoples]]' majority in the [[General Assembly of Sinchew|General Assembly]]. The Assembly can legislate; it cannot alter the instrument that constitutes the Senate, because the chamber whose powers an amendment would reduce is the chamber that must consent to it.

